## validation strategy
- Contract tests: required top-level shell, stable section ids/order, CSS blocks, index anchors.
- Section tests: cover branding, theory inserts, RH/personas trace rendering, equipment cards, alternatives, failure block.
- Asset tests: remote-logo download, local image staging, missing-image placeholder, local-only output paths.
- Integration test: `proyectos/1/input.json` + `resultados.json` + `spec.json` -> `proyectos/1/memoria.html` containing branded cover, index, RH `129.6 m3/h`, `EX-150`, `EX-160/200/250`, and local asset refs.
- Acceptance gate: browser-openable HTML with readable print styles and no missing required section.

## rollback or mitigation notes
- If KaTeX integration blocks progress, ship v1 with bundled/local KaTeX assets and limit formula conversion to supported trace patterns; always keep human fallback.
- If remote logo download is flaky in tests, mock download in unit tests and keep one controlled integration fixture.
- If project/catálogo image paths vary, centralize normalization in asset stager and fall back to placeholder instead of branching in templates.
- If generated HTML snapshot churn is too high, snapshot per section/body fragment instead of whole document while keeping one smoke golden render.

## handoff instructions for builders
- Keep section ownership file-scoped: one group per section family to avoid template/CSS collisions.
- Separate pure view-model builders from IO/staging code; templates should consume normalized local asset paths only.
- Treat `resultados.json` and `spec.json` as read-only upstream contracts; do not recalculate or reselection inside memory engine.
- Prefer deterministic placeholders, deterministic anchor ids, deterministic section ordering.
- Keep print CSS minimal in v1: readability, spacing, page-break hints only where trivial; no advanced pagination logic.
- Use `proyectos/1` as golden evidence throughout; tests must pin real values from current fixtures.

## risks and dependencies
- Biggest dependency: no existing `lib/memory-engine/` package, so poor early file ownership will cause merge collisions.
- Asset staging risk: remote logo fetch can add flaky tests and path normalization bugs.
- KaTeX risk: structured trace schema may cover only RH cleanly at first; unsupported formulas must degrade gracefully to human trace.
- Integration risk: project-local assets may not exist yet for selected models, so placeholder path must be verified early.
- Agent-doc drift risk: current prompts mention PDF; scope-limited phase must remove/override that to avoid builder overreach.
- Snapshot risk: full-document HTML can be noisy; validation should mix targeted assertions + one smoke render.
